winebus: the motor path is a question about the pad, not about the cable.

mgvf-0024 made the pad's own haptic path the default over Bluetooth and left a
cable on the legacy motors, for a reason that was honest at the time: nothing
had measured which was better on a wire, and a wired pad has none of the link
pressure that made the question worth asking again.

The trouble is what that leaves a person holding. One entry in one menu, and it
means the finer path or the harder one depending on whether a wire is plugged
in. Nobody reports that as a setting. They report it as the pad feeling
different on a cable, which is exactly the sentence that sends somebody looking
for a fault that is not there.

So both transports answer it the same way now, and the way they answer it is
the one Sony's library asks for on every packet of every session captured here.
A cable is not a different pad.

AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT MAKES THIS SAFE TO UNIFY arrived the day after
mgvf-0024, on Mortal Shell 2 -- a title that drives the pad through libScePad,
so running it twice with only the rewrite moved is the same content against
itself rather than one game against another. Same motor bytes, gain neutral:
the legacy rewrite is CLEARLY HARDER, and the haptic path is finer. Two
characters, both real, neither of them a defect of the other. That is a
preference, and a preference belongs to the person and not to the transport.

XInputRumbleHapticPath still overrides it on either transport, which is the
point: the driver picks the path Sony picks, and the menu picks for anybody who
wants the other.

NOT CHANGED: the ride. It stamps a 0x31 and a wired pad is written 0x02s, so
there is nothing on a cable to ride and its default stays what mgvf-0024 made
it. That one really is a fact about the transport.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -2298,12 +2298,17 @@
      * made that comparison unfair -- there was no hidden attenuator, the pad
      * was at full power in both.
      *
-     * A CABLE KEEPS THE LEGACY PATH. Nothing measured says which is better
-     * there, and a wired pad has none of the link pressure that made the
-     * comparison worth re-running; changing it would be a preference dressed
-     * as a finding. The option overrides either way. */
-    ext->haptics_path = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleHapticPath",
-                                          dualsense_speaks_bluetooth(ext))
+     * MGVF mgvf-0025 GAVE A CABLE THE SAME DEFAULT. mgvf-0024 left the cable on
+     * legacy for want of a measurement, which made one menu entry mean two
+     * different things depending on whether a wire was plugged in -- the kind
+     * of difference a person reports as the pad being inconsistent rather than
+     * as a setting. The path is a question about the pad's motors and not about
+     * the transport, so both transports now answer it the same way, and the way
+     * they answer it is the one Sony's library asks for on every packet.
+     *
+     * The option overrides it on either transport, and that is the point: which
+     * of the two is wanted is a preference, and the menu owns it. */
+    ext->haptics_path = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleHapticPath", 1)
                         ? DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC : DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY;
     if (ext->haptics_path == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC)
     {
